          "text": "The task: Read framer.md (done). \"What sorts of related problems are compute constrained?\" — I need to write to physical-widener.md a set of concrete options (related problems that are compute constrained, in the broadened sense defined in the prompt), and return JSON with items.\n\nThe prompt says: \"Count a related problem as compute constrained when it is still open whether more parallel compute would change its answer, not only when compute is already known to bind there. List both kinds, and mark the second kind settled in compute_open.\"\n\nSo I need related problems (from the framer.md §9 compute_signals — three of them: structure/energetics at scale, endosomal-fate biophysics, variant-effect ML over NGS). Possibly also more. The framer lists three \"compute_signals\" in §9. The prompt says \"One entry per concrete option named in the file\" — the file being... hmm, \"named in the file\" — which file? \"Use the option's heading label in your file as its id.\" — my file is physical-widener.md. So I write physical-widener.md with options as headings, then the JSON items correspond to those options. The options are the related compute-constrained problems derived from the framer.\n\nSo I should write physical-widener.md with concrete options (related problems that are compute constrained), each with a heading label, and then return JSON with id = heading label.\n\nAlso: \"Also count a problem whose answer takes long because its steps run one after another, even where more hardware would not change the answer, only how soon it arrives.\" — so sequential/latency-bound problems count too. For each option I must return `sequential` field: the chain, etc. Or \"none: ...\" if work already splits into independent pieces.\n\nEach option needs:\n- id (heading label in my file)\n- title\n- summary\n- root_link: one sentence on which part of the question in framer.md it bears on, or \"none\"\n- compute_open: one sentence naming quantity more parallel c